Federated Split BERT for Heterogeneous Text Classiﬁcation
Zhengyang Li†, Shijing Si†, Jianzong Wang∗ and Jing Xiao Ping An Technology (Shenzhen) Co., Ltd., Shenzhen, China
Email: {lizhengyang927, sishijing204, wangjianzong347, xiaojing661}@pingan.com.cn

arXiv:2205.13299v1 [cs.CL] 26 May 2022

Abstract—Pre-trained BERT models have achieved impressive performance in many natural language processing (NLP) tasks. However, in many real-world situations, textual data are usually decentralized over many clients and unable to be uploaded to a central server due to privacy protection and regulations. Federated learning (FL) enables multiple clients collaboratively to train a global model while keeping the local data privacy. A few researches have investigated BERT in federated learning setting, but the problem of performance loss caused by heterogeneous (e.g., non-IID) data over clients remain under-explored. To address this issue, we propose a framework, FedSplitBERT, which handles heterogeneous data and decreases the communication cost by splitting the BERT encoder layers into local part and global part. The local part parameters are trained by the local client only while the global part parameters are trained by aggregating gradients of multiple clients. Due to the sheer size of BERT, we explore a quantization method to further reduce the communication cost with minimal performance loss. Our framework is ready-to-use and compatible to many existing federated learning algorithms, including FedAvg, FedProx and FedAdam. Our experiments verify the effectiveness of the proposed framework, which outperforms baseline methods by a signiﬁcant margin, while FedSplitBERT with quantization can reduce the communication cost by 11.9×.
Index Terms—Federated Learning, BERT, Data Heterogeneity, Quantization, Text Classiﬁcation
I. INTRODUCTION
Lately language model pre-training has shown to be highly effective in learning universal language representations from large-scale unlabeled data. Pre-trained models such as ELMo [1], GPT [2] and BERT [3] have achieved great success in many NLP tasks, such as sentiment classiﬁcation [4], natural language inference [5], and question answering [6]. In many real-world applications, textual data such as clinical records are decentralized and stored locally on client devices [7], such as phones and personal computers. Due to the recent stringent regulation on private data protection [8], these data cannot be directly uploaded to a central server. Federated learning [9], [10] allows multiple clients to train a global deep neural network model collaboratively in a distributed environment without moving data to a centralized storage.
There have been some attempts on ﬁne-tuning BERT in federated learning setting. For example, [11] proposed an encryption method for clients to protect data privacy while
